-- title:
--   Finiteness of residually transcendental valuation subrings over A
-- statement:
--   Throughout, $C$ denotes the $A$-subalgebra `chartAlgFin A ↥K j` of $K$, that is the set of elements of $K$ integral over $A[j]$ (the $j$-finite chart algebra), $\mathfrak X$ denotes the two-chart integral model [`AlgebraicCurve.TwoChartIntegralModel A ↥K j`](def/AlgebraicCurve_TwoChartIntegralModel.html#L236), the pushout gluing $\operatorname{Spec}$ of the integral closure of $A[j]$ to $\operatorname{Spec}$ of the integral closure of $A[j^{-1}]$ in $K$, with its structure morphism `toBase` to $\operatorname{Spec} A$ and the morphism `ιFin` from $X_{\mathrm{fin}} = \operatorname{Spec} C$; and, for $\delta \in \mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbb Z)$, an $L$-algebra automorphism $\tau$ of $K$ is called a *level automorphism at $\delta$* when [`ModularCurve.FullLevel.IsLevelAutAt L (q*ℓ) ξ (q*ℓ) ((q*ℓ)^2*M') (ModularCurve.FullLevel.levelH (q*ℓ) M') δ K τ`](def/ModularCurve_FullLevelLevelAutAt.html#L29) holds, i.e. when for every quotient of integral $q$-expansions of two modular forms of equal weight for the level group attached to $N_0 = (q\ell)^2M'$ and to the subgroup of units congruent to $1$ modulo $q\ell$, the series of $\tau$ applied to that quotient is, read through any ring homomorphism $\iota : L \to \mathbb C$ with $\iota(\xi) = e^{2\pi i/(q\ell)}$, the corresponding quotient of the $q$-expansions of the forms slashed by `conjElemN (q*ℓ) δ`.
--
--   **Data.** Primes $q \ge 5$ and $\ell \ge 3$ with $\ell \ne q$; a non-zero natural number $M'$ divisible by neither $q$ nor $\ell$; a field $L$ of characteristic zero and a primitive $(q\ell)$-th root of unity $\xi \in L$; the hypothesis `hι` supplies a ring homomorphism $L \to \mathbb C$ carrying $\xi$ to $e^{2\pi i/(q\ell)}$. An intermediate field $K$ of $L \subseteq L((t))$ is pinned by `hK` to be [`ModularCurve.laurentBaseChange L`](def/ModularCurve_LaurentCoeff.html#L103) applied to [`ModularCurve.xHFunctionField ((q*ℓ)^2*M') (ModularCurve.FullLevel.levelH (q*ℓ) M')`](def/ModularCurve_XH.html#L79): the $L$-subfield of the Laurent series field generated by the coefficientwise images of the $q$-expansion function field of the congruence group of level $N_0 = (q\ell)^2M'$ attached to the subgroup $H = \ker\big((\mathbb Z/N_0)^\times \to (\mathbb Z/q\ell)^\times\big)$ of units congruent to $1$ modulo $q\ell$. Next, a domain $A$ which is a discrete valuation ring, henselian local with algebraically closed residue field, equipped with an algebra structure on $L$ making $L$ its fraction field and compatibly on $K$; `hAq` places $q$ in the maximal ideal of $A$ and `hξA` puts $\xi$ in the image of $A$. An element $j \in K$, non-zero, whose Laurent series is by `hj` the coefficientwise image of the $q$-expansion [`ModularCurve.jq`](def/ModularCurve_X0.html#L157) of the modular $j$-function. A uniformiser $\varpi$ with `hϖ` : $\mathfrak m_A = (\varpi)$, and an element $\varpi_t$ with `hϖt` : $\varpi_t^{\,q^2-1} = q\,u$ for some unit $u$ of $A$.
--
--   **The supersingular maximal ideal.** A maximal ideal $y$ of $C$ (`hy`) containing the image of $\varpi$ (`hϖy`) and satisfying `hss`: for every algebraically closed field $\Omega$ of characteristic $q$ and every ring homomorphism $\varphi : C \to \Omega$ with kernel $y$, the value $\varphi(j)$ lies in [`ModularCurve.ssJSet q Ω`](def/ModularCurve_SupersingularModuli.html#L7), i.e. every elliptic curve over $\Omega$ with that $j$-invariant has no non-zero point annihilated by $q$.
--
--   **The rigid chart hypothesis `hArig`.** For every point $z$ of $\mathfrak X$ such that the germ at $z$ of the global section obtained from $\varpi$ through `toBase` lies in the maximal ideal of the stalk, and every point $y'$ of $X_{\mathrm{fin}}$ with `ιFin` $(y') = z$ whose ideal satisfies the supersingularity condition of `hss`, there exist a domain $W$ which is a discrete valuation ring, complete for its maximal-ideal-adic topology, a ring homomorphism $\sigma : A \to W$ with $\mathfrak m_W = (\sigma\varpi)$, power series $f,u,v \in W[[X_0,X_1]]$ with $u,v$ units and $f \equiv X_0X_1^q - X_0^qX_1 \pmod{(X_0,X_1)^{q+2}}$ (the Drinfeld form [`DrinfeldCurve.LocalChart.drinfeldForm`](def/DrinfeldCurve_LocalChart.html#L18)), and a ring isomorphism $e$ from the adic completion of the stalk $\mathcal O_{\mathfrak X,z}$ onto $S = W[[X_0,X_1]]/\big(\sigma(\varpi_t^{\,q+1})v - fu\big)$, satisfying seven clauses (summarised here): the image under $e$ of the germ of a section coming from $a \in A$ is the class of the constant $\sigma(a)$; for $\gamma \in \Gamma_0(M')$, every level automorphism at $\gamma^{-1}$ maps $C$ into $C$; for $\gamma \in \Gamma_0(M') \cap \Gamma(\ell)$ such an automorphism is congruent to the identity on $C$ modulo $y'$; for $\gamma \in \Gamma_0(M')$ and a level automorphism at $\gamma^{-1}$ preserving $C$ and congruent to the identity modulo $y'$ there are a ring automorphism $\theta$ of $S$ transporting the action on germs through $e$, an element $c \in W$ and a matrix $M$ over $W$ with $\theta$ fixing constants, $\theta(X_j) \equiv \sum_i M_{ij}X_i$ modulo the square of the ideal generated by the classes of $X_0,X_1$, $c^{q+1} \equiv 1$ and $M_{ij} \equiv c\,\gamma_{ij}$ modulo $\mathfrak m_W$, with $c \equiv 1$ when $\gamma \in \Gamma(\ell)$ and $c \not\equiv 1$ when $\gamma \in \Gamma(q)$ and the automorphism is not the identity; a branch-separation clause asserting that two primes of $S$ containing the class of $\sigma\varpi$, not containing both classes of $X_0,X_1$, and containing elements with linear parts $(a_1,b_1)$, $(a_2,b_2)$ with $q \nmid a_1b_2 - a_2b_1$ have distinct contractions to the stalk; a coefficient criterion identifying, for the prime with linear part $(1,0)$, membership of the germ of $a \in C$ in its contraction with the condition that every Laurent coefficient of $a$ lie in the image of $\mathfrak m_A$; and a final clause providing that [`ModularCurve.jqNModC L (q*ℓ)`](def/ModularCurve_JqCoeff.html#L18) lies in $K$ and in $C$, an $a_0 \in A$ with the difference in $y'$, an integer $e_0 \ge 1$ and $h \in (X_0,X_1)^{e_0}$ whose degree-$e_0$ form takes unit values at all pairs $(a,b)$ in $W$ with $a$ or $b$ a unit and $a^qb - ab^q \in \mathfrak m_W$, such that the image of that difference under $e$ is the class of $h$.
--
--   **A chosen point and chart presentation.** A point $z$ with germ condition as above, a point $y'$ of $X_{\mathrm{fin}}$ over $z$ with the supersingularity condition `hss'`, and `hy'y` : the ideal of $y'$ equals $y$. Further, a complete discrete valuation domain $W_1$, a homomorphism $\sigma_1 : A \to W_1$ with $\mathfrak m_{W_1} = (\sigma_1\varpi)$, power series $f_1,u_1,v_1$ with $u_1,v_1$ units and $f_1$ congruent to the Drinfeld form modulo $(X_0,X_1)^{q+2}$, and an isomorphism $e_1$ from the completed stalk at $z$ onto $S_1 = W_1[[X_0,X_1]]/(\sigma_1(\varpi_t^{\,q+1})v_1 - f_1u_1)$; the hypothesis `hW₁` asserts for this particular witness exactly the seven clauses just listed.
--
--   **Centre, blow-up algebra and valuation subring.** An ideal $J$ of $C$ pinned by `hJ` to be the infimum of the ideals obtained, as $\gamma$ runs over $\Gamma(q) \cap \Gamma_0(M')$ and $\tau$ over the level automorphisms at $\gamma^{-1}$ preserving $C$, as the preimage under the restriction of $\tau$ to $C$ of the contraction along $e_1$ composed with completion and the germ map at $z$ of the ideal of $S_1$ generated by the classes of $\sigma_1\varpi_t$, $X_0$ and $X_1$. An $A$-subalgebra $B$ of $K$ pinned by `hB` to be $C\big[\{x \in K : x\varpi_t \in J\}\big]$, viewed over $A$. A valuation subring `W` of $K$ (this binder names a valuation subring, not a coefficient ring of a chart presentation) with `hBW` : $B \subseteq$ `W`. The hypothesis `hR1` asserts $C \subseteq B$ and that every element of $K$ is a quotient $g/h$ with $g,h \in B$, $h \ne 0$. The hypothesis `hR2` asserts that $A \to B$ is formally smooth and of finite presentation and that $B/\varpi B$ has Krull dimension at most $1$. The hypothesis `hR3` (five clauses) asserts that an element of $L$ lies in `W` exactly when it comes from $A$; that $\mathfrak m_{\mathrm{W}}$ is generated by the image of $\varpi$; that `W` is a discrete valuation ring; that for $b \in C$ membership in $y$ is equivalent to membership in $\mathfrak m_{\mathrm{W}}$; and that $f \in K$ lies in `W` exactly when $f = g/h$ with $g,h \in B$ and $h \notin \mathfrak m_{\mathrm{W}}$. The hypothesis `hEQ` (five clauses, summarised here) asserts: for every algebra structure of $\mathbb F_{q^2}$ on the residue field $k$ of $A$ there is a surjective ring homomorphism $\rho$ from $B$ onto the Drinfeld coordinate ring [`DrinfeldCurve.CoordRing q k`](def/DrinfeldCurve_CoordRing.html#L21) whose kernel is $B \cap \mathfrak m_{\mathrm{W}}$, which is compatible with $A \to k$, and which, for $\gamma \in \Gamma_0(M')$ and a level automorphism at $\gamma^{-1}$ leaving `W` stable, intertwines that automorphism with the action `hAction` of a pair $(\gamma \bmod q, c)$ lying in `hSubgroup q` (the kernel of $(g,c)\mapsto \det(g)\,c^{q+1}$), with $c \ne 1$ whenever $\gamma \in \Gamma(q)$ and the automorphism is not the identity; that level automorphisms at $\gamma^{-1}$ for $\gamma \in \Gamma_0(M')$ preserve $B$; that every prime of $B$ containing the image of $\varpi$ receives $B \cap \mathfrak m_{\mathrm{W}}$ under some level automorphism at $\gamma^{-1}$ with $\gamma \in \Gamma(q) \cap \Gamma_0(M')$; that an element of $B$ mapped into $\mathfrak m_{\mathrm{W}}$ by all such automorphisms is divisible by $\varpi$ in $B$; and that a level automorphism at $\gamma^{-1}$, $\gamma \in \Gamma_0(M')$, which preserves $y$ on $C$ also preserves `W`.
--
--   **Conclusion.** The set of valuation subrings $V$ of $K$ satisfying the following three conditions is finite: (i) for every $x \in L$, the image of $x$ in $K$ lies in $V$ if and only if $x$ lies in the image of $A$; (ii) the image of $\varpi$ in $K$ lies in $V$ and lies in the maximal ideal of $V$; (iii) $j$ lies in $V$ and, for every monic polynomial $p$ over $A$, the value at $j$ of the image of $p$ in $K[X]$ lies in $V$ and does not lie in the maximal ideal of $V$. Condition (iii) says that the residue of $j$ in the residue field of $V$ is a root of no monic polynomial over the residue field of $A$, so that the $V$ counted are the Igusa-type, residually transcendental extensions of $A$; the conclusion involves only $L$, $K$, $A$, $j$ and $\varpi$, the remaining data recording the geometric situation in which the statement is applied.
--
--   A finiteness statement for the valuation subrings of the rigid-level modular function field $K$ that lie over the discrete valuation ring $A$, have $\varpi$ in their maximal ideal, and on which $j$ is integral with all monic values units — the Gauss, or Igusa-type, valuations attached to components of the special fibre of the $j$-finite chart. It is used in the end-pole analysis of the two-chart integral model, where finiteness converts pointwise pole witnesses at such valuations into a single function on the blow-up chart.
